course: dictionary
lesson: 4864
title: Icelandic: a place and a language
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Icelandic is a word you can use in two ways. It can describe something from Iceland, an island country in the North Atlantic, like «an Icelandic ash cloud». It can also name the language spoken there. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning is being used.
explain: «A volcano's reach can be enormous: Krakatoa in 1883 was heard nearly 5,000 km away, Laki in 1783-84 poisoned the animals of Iceland, and in 2010 an Icelandic ash cloud stopped most flights in Europe for about a week.» Here Icelandic tells you where the ash cloud came from. The sentence is about how far a volcano can reach, so the word helps you picture Iceland's volcanoes.
explain: Context clues can also tell you that a word is a describing word. In «an Icelandic horse», the word Icelandic describes the horse, so it is an adjective. In «She reads sagas in Icelandic», it names a language, so it works like a noun. Try swapping in another word, such as Danish, and see whether the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Icelandic means from or to do with Iceland, an island country in the North Atlantic.
fact: Icelandic is also the language spoken in Iceland.
fact: Iceland has volcanoes, glaciers, hot springs and geysers, so its landscapes are a favourite example in geography.
fact: The Icelandic language is close to Old Norse, the language of the Vikings, so Icelanders can still read their old sagas.
fact: Icelandic horses are small and sturdy. Many Icelandic names end in -son or -dóttir, which show a father's or mother's name, and -dóttir means daughter of.
exercise: In «an Icelandic ash cloud», what does Icelandic tell you about the cloud? | It was made of ice | It was very cold | *It came from Iceland | It was a kind of cake
exercise: Which is the language spoken in Iceland? | Swiss German | Latin | *Icelandic | Old Norse, which is spoken there every day
exercise: Icelandic names often end in -dóttir. What does -dóttir mean? | son of | a town in the hills | *daughter of | a horse with a white mane
test: Which sentence uses Icelandic as a describing word? | The class learned Icelandic at school | *Lena rode a small, sturdy Icelandic horse | She reads sagas written in Icelandic | Icelandic is spoken on an island
test: In «Krakatoa in 1883 was heard nearly 5,000 km away», what is the sentence mainly about? | How to learn a language | Icelandic horses | Krakatoa's ships | *How far a volcano's sound can travel
exam: Icelandic can mean a place or a language. Which sentence uses it for the language? | Ari visited an Icelandic volcano last summer | Ari's horse is a small Icelandic pony | Ari lives in Iceland, an island in the North Atlantic | *Ari reads his grandmother's sagas in Icelandic
